  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively show that heating HEAs, including under vacuum, can cause phase instability, element-dependent diffusion, outward migration, surface depletion or enrichment, and selective evaporation. R9 and R16 are especially relevant to preferential outward diffusion during high-temperature exposure, while R5 supplies a related selective-evaporation mechanism. However, none addresses AuCoCuNiPt nanoparticles, surface-energy-driven Au/Cu segregation, non-sluggish diffusion linked to nanoparticle reshaping, or the associated size and shape evolution. Thus the literature points toward thermally activated compositional redistribution and loss, but reconstructing the specific nanoparticle intervention and surface-energy mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
